<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=bayonet">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayonet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1606">1606</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as plug bayonet, socket bayonet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A bayonet is a knife, dagger, sword or spike mounted on the barrel of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> or rifle, so that the gun can be used as a spear in close combat. The first recorded bayonet proper is the 'gun-knife' of the son-and-mother gun in the Chinese military treatise Binglu of 1606, a plug bayonet pushed into the barrel. Jean Martinet standardized plug bayonets for new French fusilier regiments in 1671; because they stopped the gun from firing, socket bayonets that fitted around the barrel replaced them, and by the mid-18th century most European armies had adopted these for massed infantry charges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayonet.jpg" alt="Bayonet" width="330" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auge=mit · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US-Bajonette_verschiedener_Epochen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their rising of 1820 forced Ferdinand I to grant the Spanish constitution of 1812 on 13 July, which he cancelled on 23 February 1821 before Austrian <b>bayonets</b>; repression then deepened.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>On Hoover's orders, troops under Douglas MacArthur used tear gas and <b>bayonets</b> to clear camps of First World War veterans demanding early payment of bonuses.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-28">28 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1606</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The word bayonette is older, from the 16th century, but it is unclear whether it then named a knife fitted to a gun; its supposed origin in the town of Bayonne has no basis in fact.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Musket<span>by 1521</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=bayonet">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
